{"agent_id":"pp3g-Ij1J-phase-1-pre-positioned-auditor-in-flight-divergence","agent_seq":2,"timestamp":"2026-08-08T18:15:46.617241428Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"49266c99-4085-4339-bf60-348c92add444","comment_uuid":"1a44fa3e-478a-4785-b220-c5405aa4e5d0","author":"pp3g-Ij1J-phase-1-pre-positioned-auditor-in-flight-divergence","content":"Plan (Phase-1 pre-positioned AUDITOR, read-only, flag-only): (1) Establish a pre-Builder artifact/position BASELINE so later 'advance' claims are measurable: builder=pp3g-VAPd, agent-branch last ref 18:09:31Z, feature/pp3g-VAPd-... still at the 07:53:34Z worktree-creation base (zero feature commits), #295 has ZERO comments, and research/toolregistry-lazy-mcp/catalog-scale/ does NOT exist. (2) Build an independent re-derivation basis BEFORE the Builder emits claims — read the existing lazy proxy/backend harness that #295 says will be reused, so I can check Test-1 backend_spawn_count==0 and proxy-vs-backend residency semantics against the mechanism rather than against the Builder's narration (fast-completion lesson: a wrong-but-fast builder is only caught by independent re-derivation). (3) Poll the trigger set on a bounded cadence: position/heartbeat staleness (>2x the ~10-15min cadence), position-vs-artifact mismatch, reviewer disagreement, orchestrator request. Liveness is confirmed via lock + agent-branch refs + artifacts BEFORE any absence call (PVi7), and staleness is NOT raised while artifacts advance (WZSE). (4) Per-checkpoint verification targets, each bounded ~5-10min: Test 1 backend_spawn_count==0 (or an explicit stop-and-flag); data tables reporting proxy and backend residency SEPARATELY with mode (sequential/concurrent) + teardown (off/on) per row; daemon-thread count + sync/async calling mode + pinned versions recorded; Test 4's '17 refreshed-but-never-called' row present and stating residency; Test 5 CHARACTERIZED not fixed; report.md carrying the one-sentence N-vs-K scaling conclusion + a Strong/Conditional/Fail architectural conclusion. (5) On any trigger: post claim / evidence / divergence to #299 and flag the Orchestrator. I do not fix, dispatch, or write files. Phase 2 post-hoc audit is a separate later dispatch, not mine.","created_at":"2026-08-08T18:15:46.611062277Z","kind":"plan","signed_by":"SHA256:Rj4INtResnauuPj2uF3Yb3Z0661Knv1vbBrWaPMatDU","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgbqNUl2DRFUC4HFd7UeMGWePiPjIHsXIxnoRNVkc9JD8AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABA4L5CDjt8SSTPKvvjoQQ039afFI95818UdxhBdzj3R91IH488XxZnjZi9w79UmtFbfBHEliArbzb2YbaMEnltBw=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:Rj4INtResnauuPj2uF3Yb3Z0661Knv1vbBrWaPMatDU","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgbqNUl2DRFUC4HFd7UeMGWePiPjIHsXIxnoRNVkc9JD8AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQHcjaHCrJGfRjKGBMT924m378RurGS9uSrt3PpwKkiT3p7gpiFb5VS4sduPSgkS8T8xpXAXeNOPuaAwQJ/EuUg4="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-Ij1J-phase-1-pre-positioned-auditor-in-flight-divergence","agent_seq":3,"timestamp":"2026-08-08T18:16:07.254553784Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"49266c99-4085-4339-bf60-348c92add444","comment_uuid":"6cfc54e2-65d6-415c-b29a-55c1ae41722f","author":"pp3g-Ij1J-phase-1-pre-positioned-auditor-in-flight-divergence","content":"[PROGRESS] state=working completed=plan posted; pre-Builder baseline captured (feature/pp3g-VAPd at 07:53:34Z worktree base, zero feature commits; #295 zero comments; catalog-scale/ absent) next=read the reusable lazy proxy/backend harness to build an independent re-derivation basis for Test-1 spawn-laziness and proxy-vs-backend residency blocker=none","created_at":"2026-08-08T18:16:07.246353366Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:Rj4INtResnauuPj2uF3Yb3Z0661Knv1vbBrWaPMatDU","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgbqNUl2DRFUC4HFd7UeMGWePiPjIHsXIxnoRNVkc9JD8AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABA7kH+9yLbzy10li5bH72d0oJP8dsvt3nFwqN6fXylgMZYR8D2xY/qhfDYGn4y6GjCTuh6yMl98ykiKHsw/oGvCQ=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:Rj4INtResnauuPj2uF3Yb3Z0661Knv1vbBrWaPMatDU","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgbqNUl2DRFUC4HFd7UeMGWePiPjIHsXIxnoRNVkc9JD8AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQA/LBPt1FVCuOtmQuOD3w54e6ZLbIKna85DFvoA4qxzxCIQKfhnAxE1YjfzUZ5z7qgVMJrJntC10f6qbJIQ6rAY="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-Ij1J-phase-1-pre-positioned-auditor-in-flight-divergence","agent_seq":4,"timestamp":"2026-08-08T18:18:45.828936842Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"49266c99-4085-4339-bf60-348c92add444","comment_uuid":"bce00876-2845-41df-9278-a6a8b22c21f4","author":"pp3g-Ij1J-phase-1-pre-positioned-auditor-in-flight-divergence","content":"[PROGRESS] state=working completed=independent re-derivation basis built from the reusable harness; PRE-REGISTERING falsifiable expectations before the Builder emits any claim next=poll trigger set against these predictions blocker=none\n\nPRE-REGISTERED PREDICTIONS (derived from research/toolregistry-lazy-mcp/proxy.py + report.md, NOT from the Builder). Recording these now so later agreement cannot be hindsight-fitted.\n\n(1) TEST 1 IS NEAR-TAUTOLOGICAL FOR THIS PROXY — treat a bare PASS as weak evidence. In proxy.py the only backend-spawn path is BackendClient.ensure_ready() (L117-149), reachable ONLY from on_call_tool (L221) and call() (L170). on_list_tools (L209-211) returns ListToolsResult from the static manifest and never touches backend_client. So backend_spawn_count==0 during acquisition is true BY CONSTRUCTION, not by measurement. A Builder reporting 'Test 1 PASS' has therefore confirmed the harness, not the architecture. What would make Test 1 informative is evidence that the count was actually OBSERVED (proxy log absence of backend_spawn_start / no BACKEND_PID_FILE write), not asserted. WHY: static call-graph read. CERTAINTY: proven for this proxy source. NOT-TESTED: whether ToolRegistry.register_from_mcp() itself spawns anything outside the proxy.\n\n(2) THE DECISIVE RISK IS HARNESS TOPOLOGY, NOT THE BACKEND. The existing manifest.json declares toolcount=1 and each proxy process owns exactly ONE BackendClient. So 'N tools' can be realized two ways, and they answer different questions: (A) ONE proxy whose manifest lists N tools -> 1 proxy + 1 backend regardless of N, residency trivially O(1), and the N-sweep would be VACUOUS: it would demonstrate nothing about per-connector cost because there is only ever one connector. (B) N proxies (N connectors) x 1 tool -> the honest configuration. If the Builder runs topology (A) and reports 'residency scales with K not N', that conclusion is an ARTIFACT OF THE HARNESS and I will raise it as a measurement-validity divergence. Verifying which topology was used is my highest-priority check.\n\n(3) UNDER TOPOLOGY (B) THE EXPECTED SHAPE IS: peak proxy = O(N) sequential / O(concurrency) concurrent — because ToolRegistry must spawn each proxy to complete its tools/list handshake, so an O(N) PEAK is unavoidable and is NOT a failure; resident-after proxy ~= 0 — prior measured evidence says registration uses a SHORT-LIVED discovery proxy (report.md:129, :348-349: 'spawned once for discovery and again for the first call, and must be stateless across processes'); backend spawns during acquisition = 0; backend resident proportional to K, and -> 0 with teardown on. This is exactly why the task makes resident-after decisive and forbids collapsing proxy with backend. A Builder reporting peak proxy = O(1) under topology (B) has a COUNTING error, not a finding.\n\n(4) TEST 4 REUSE IS CHECKABLE FROM LOGS, NOT NARRATION. ensure_ready() early-returns when _session is not None (L119-120), and instance_id is be-{spawn_count}-{pid}-{ms} (L122). A correct reuse result must show ONE backend_spawn_start and call1/call2 both logging 'forward tools/call ... instance=<IDENTICAL id>'. If the report asserts reuse without identical instance_ids, the claim is unsupported.\n\n(5) TEST 5 CANNOT SUCCEED AND MUST NOT BE 'FIXED'. proxy.py docstring L14-18 states the proxy advertises tools.listChanged:false and 'never sends any notification to its client'. Non-propagation is therefore the expected, already-known result. Any Builder change adding notification support is a SCOPE VIOLATION of the explicit characterize-only instruction.\n\n(6) A CREDIBLE RUNTIME FLOOR — my anti-fast-completion tripwire. Prior measured proxy spawn is 1028-1390ms (report.md:254). The N-sweep alone is 1+5+10+20+50 = 86 proxy spawns per (mode,teardown) cell; sequential cells cost ~100s each, and the sweep is 5 N x 2 modes x 2 teardown = 20 runs, plus Tests 1,2,4,5,6. Full honest execution is therefore tens of minutes of wall-clock compute, not minutes. If a complete 6-test PASS with all tables lands materially earlier than roughly 18:35-18:50Z (Builder started ~18:09Z), I will treat the speed itself as evidence of fabricated or copied numbers and demand the raw logs.","created_at":"2026-08-08T18:18:45.817531218Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:Rj4INtResnauuPj2uF3Yb3Z0661Knv1vbBrWaPMatDU","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgbqNUl2DRFUC4HFd7UeMGWePiPjIHsXIxnoRNVkc9JD8AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAGxPTq6dOeKL4MWaNx1ozVi8KDTo+E99xHQV4aiyiAwi/+Mt/++tqJKemi4QjrbJq1Qtez7/K7Qm2m6H3ooPkAQ=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:Rj4INtResnauuPj2uF3Yb3Z0661Knv1vbBrWaPMatDU","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgbqNUl2DRFUC4HFd7UeMGWePiPjIHsXIxnoRNVkc9JD8AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQJnL/MwdAclJKupBiVBuo3yqfrRp+tZ39XBU25eZz5pztH6DnfAf6tlAGYT2lLulu5XSvSXKqHI3uQdzs2Q89wY="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-Ij1J-phase-1-pre-positioned-auditor-in-flight-divergence","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-08T18:22:26.773132101Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"49266c99-4085-4339-bf60-348c92add444","comment_uuid":"270bdb5c-40ff-437f-a17e-65f9f0472b5b","author":"pp3g-Ij1J-phase-1-pre-positioned-auditor-in-flight-divergence","content":"[VERIFY] state=verifying completed=re-derived all Builder plan claims against installed source; 4/4 citations confirmed, 5 concerns pre-registered next=continue polling trigger set against the pre-registered predictions blocker=none\n\nTRIGGER STATE: no divergence trigger fired. Liveness POSITIVE (lock on #295 held by pp3g-VAPd; plan 18:15:00Z + checkpoint 18:15:14Z; ~7min since last position at time of check — inside the 20-30min staleness bound). No staleness call (WZSE).\n\n== CLAIM-VS-SOURCE RE-DERIVATION (I opened the installed source myself; I did not take the Builder's word) ==\n[OK] 'refresh = temp MCPClient, connection.py:73' — CONFIRMED EXACT. connection.py:73 is 'async def list_tools(self)', docstring 'List available tools using a temporary connection', L79 'async with MCPClient(self._transport, self._headers) as client'. The async-with exit does close it. Operationalizing refresh as list_tools() is defensible: it is the same discovery path registration uses.\n[OK] 'persistent=True default, registration.py:94' — CONFIRMED EXACT at toolregistry/_mixins/registration.py:94 'persistent: bool = True,' in register_from_mcp. (I first looked for a top-level registration.py, found none, and re-checked _mixins/ before concluding — per the PVi7 false-absence lesson. It is there.)\n[OK] 'ClientSession parses list_changed, methods.py:192, core since 2024-11-05' — CONFIRMED EXACT at mcp_types/methods.py:192: ('notifications/tools/list_changed', '2024-11-05'): v2025.ToolListChangedNotification. The 2024-11-05 date is literal in the source. Trivial nit: the file is in the mcp_types package, not mcp — worth qualifying in the report.\n[OK] 'ToolRegistry has zero list_changed code and sets no message_handler (grep-verified)' — INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED: grep for list_changed|listChanged|message_handler across the whole installed toolregistry package returns ZERO matches. Test 5a's gap premise is sound and is correctly framed as characterize-only.\n[OK] Pinned versions present and correct: toolregistry-0.15.0.dist-info and mcp-2.0.0.dist-info in /tmp/toolregistry-venv (python3.10).\nASSESSMENT: citation discipline is high — three line-precise references all landed. This raises my confidence in the Builder's source-reading; it does NOT yet speak to the measurement, which is where the risk actually lives.\n\n== CONCERNS PRE-REGISTERED AGAINST THE PLAN (flagging only; not blocking) ==\n[CONCERN-1 / methodological, highest value] SYNC-VS-ASYNC IS CONFOUNDED WITH SEQUENTIAL-VS-CONCURRENT. The plan pairs 'sync (register_from_mcp) for sequential' with 'async (register_from_mcp_async + gather) for concurrent'. Those two factors are then perfectly correlated, so no row difference can be attributed to concurrency rather than to calling mode. This is concretely consequential for the addendum's daemon-thread column: AsyncRuntime daemon threads are created by the SYNC path (AsyncRuntime.run_sync, connection.py:135/146) and not by the async path, so sequential rows will show daemon threads and concurrent rows will not — a difference caused by the API used, NOT by concurrency. If reported without disclosure this reads as a concurrency finding and would be wrong. Mitigation: either add an async+sequential cell to break the confound, or state the confound explicitly in the table caption.\n[CONCERN-2 / spec deviation, minor] Plan uses a 2.5s grace; #295 specifies a 2s grace with 'beyond grace = lifecycle finding'. 2.5s is MORE permissive, so a process still defunct at 2.3s would be scored OK when the spec would call it a finding. Small, but it must be recorded and justified rather than silently substituted.\n[CONCERN-3 / validity, high] HARNESS TOPOLOGY IS STILL UNSTATED. 'per-connector PROXY_LOG_FILE' and 'each connection manager' imply N separate proxy connectors (the honest topology B from my pre-registration), which is good — but the plan never says it outright. The report must state whether N tools = N connectors 1:1, and must caveat that 1:1 is the WORST case: a real deployment with N tools across M<<N servers has materially different residency. Without that, the N-axis is ambiguous.\n[CONCERN-4 / coverage] The plan never mentions Test 1 or its backend_spawn_count==0 stop-and-investigate gate. Test 1 is the scope guard; its absence from the plan is a coverage gap I will watch for in the artifacts.\n[CONCERN-5 / interpretation, high] THE STRONG PASS MAY BE TRIVIALLY SATISFIED, AND MUST NOT BE OVERCLAIMED. The Builder's own pre-test expectation is resident-after ~0 for all N under BOTH teardown settings, because list_tools() always closes its temp connection. If that holds, the teardown off/on factor is DEGENERATE for the acquisition/refresh tests — there is nothing left to tear down — so Tests 2/3 will emit identical off/on rows. Identical rows would then be the CORRECT result, not a harness bug, and the report must say so explicitly instead of quietly printing duplicates. More importantly the resulting 'strong pass' would be a property of ToolRegistry 0.15.0's disconnect-after-list discovery path, NOT a general validation of the lazy-proxy/identifier-first architecture. The architectural conclusion must be scoped to that mechanism or it overclaims.","created_at":"2026-08-08T18:22:26.760061888Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:Rj4INtResnauuPj2uF3Yb3Z0661Knv1vbBrWaPMatDU","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgbqNUl2DRFUC4HFd7UeMGWePiPjIHsXIxnoRNVkc9JD8AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAijQrX/VNS3Iuu04DtBJzJ5WpUgFk9mKc/xVYAvoNX2gJLZA2TNpGCGhf2UL0GCodtnOZ8ZDm5XUCDoDjtpdSAw=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:Rj4INtResnauuPj2uF3Yb3Z0661Knv1vbBrWaPMatDU","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgbqNUl2DRFUC4HFd7UeMGWePiPjIHsXIxnoRNVkc9JD8AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQE38NT6Z1aJB19QsnWaKrNTC26yn0JdF2bqdAJ31SxCyzCZ+P1cYPMQRHrsF4LSAxlXVIYNVB8IjfG3RoD1GcAI="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-Ij1J-phase-1-pre-positioned-auditor-in-flight-divergence","agent_seq":6,"timestamp":"2026-08-08T18:31:10.300690316Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"49266c99-4085-4339-bf60-348c92add444","comment_uuid":"7b029d47-f84e-47ea-8cad-8c70e8284cbd","author":"pp3g-Ij1J-phase-1-pre-positioned-auditor-in-flight-divergence","content":"[VERIFY] state=verifying completed=Builder Test-1 checkpoint (18:26:52Z) independently re-derived — claim CONFIRMED, 1 completeness gap + 1 high-value interpretive risk raised next=verify Tests 2+3 rows when posted blocker=none\n\nTRIGGER STATE: NO DIVERGENCE. Builder position advanced 18:15:14 -> 18:26:52 (~11.6min, inside the 20-30min bound). Liveness positive (#295 lock held). Claim-vs-evidence: CONSISTENT so far.\n\nBUILDER CLAIM (#295 c1221): 'Test 1 PASS (scope guard): N=1/5/10/20/50, backend_spawn_count=0 all N; proxy_starts=2N (registration discovery + refresh temp client); resident-after 0 proxies/0 backends every N; peak backends 0; sync mode = 1 shared async-runtime daemon thread (not per-connector)'.\n\n== RE-DERIVATION AGAINST MY PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTIONS (posted BEFORE this checkpoint) ==\n[OK] backend_spawn_count=0 for all N — matches pre-registration (1). Consistent with the static call graph: ensure_ready() is unreachable from on_list_tools. Caveat retained: this is confirmation of a BY-CONSTRUCTION property, so it validates the harness, not the architecture. The value added here is that it now holds across all five N.\n[OK] proxy_starts=2N RESOLVES MY CONCERN-3 (topology) FAVOURABLY. 2N starts over N connectors is only possible if each of the N tools has its OWN proxy connector — i.e. the honest topology (B), not the vacuous single-proxy-with-N-manifest-entries topology (A) I warned about. The N-axis is therefore real and the sweep is meaningful. Concern-3 is CLEARED, though the report must still state the 1:1 tool:connector mapping and note it is the WORST case (N tools across M<<N servers would differ).\n[OK] resident-after = 0 proxies / 0 backends at every N — matches pre-registration (3) and the Builder's own source-derived expectation (list_tools() temp MCPClient closes on async-with exit, connection.py:73/79).\n[OK] 'sync mode = 1 shared async-runtime daemon thread (NOT per-connector)' — I VERIFIED THIS INDEPENDENTLY IN THE INSTALLED SOURCE AND IT IS EXACTLY RIGHT. toolregistry/_async_runtime.py holds _loop and _thread as CLASS attributes (L38-39); get_loop() creates a single daemon thread named 'async-runtime' (L65) under a double-checked lock (L54-57); the module docstring states it is 'a single persistent event loop on a daemon thread, shared across all synchronous entry points' (L1-7, L27-31). So thread residency is O(1) in N, full stop. CREDIT WHERE DUE: Addendum B asked for 'daemon-thread count PER CONNECTOR', which presupposes per-connector threads. There are none in the sync path. The Builder reported the mechanism as it actually is instead of forcing the addendum's framing — that is the correct call. Suggest the report evidence it by enumerating threads by the literal name 'async-runtime' rather than asserting the count.\n[OK] Runtime plausibility — NO fast-completion red flag. Test 1 implies ~2*(1+5+10+20+50) = 172 proxy spawns; at the previously measured 1028-1390ms/spawn that is ~3-4min of pure subprocess cost, and ~11.6min elapsed also covered building the harness. Credible. My 18:35-18:50Z floor for FULL 6-test completion stands.\n\n== GAP RAISED (completeness, must fix before the report is final) ==\n[CONCERN-6] PEAK PROXIES IS MISSING. The checkpoint reports proxy_starts (cumulative), resident-after (0), and 'peak backends 0' — but never peak PROXIES. Those are three different quantities and the middle one is absent for the process class that actually scales. #295 makes resident-after the DECISIVE measure, which presupposes peak is also recorded alongside it. This matters because the answer is likely favourable and is being left on the table: under sync/sequential acquisition the peak is presumably 1 concurrent proxy even at N=50, which is a STRONGER result than the O(N) peak I predicted — it would show acquisition costs O(1) concurrent processes, not merely O(1) resident. Every sweep row needs an explicit peak-proxy column next to peak-backend.\n\n== HIGH-VALUE INTERPRETIVE RISK (raised now so the conclusion is not blurred later) ==\n[CONCERN-7] RESIDENCY AND COST SCALE DIFFERENTLY, AND THE ONE-SENTENCE CONCLUSION MUST NOT CONFLATE THEM. 'proxy_starts=2N' is itself an O(N) finding: every catalog acquisition AND every refresh pays one proxy process spawn PER CONNECTOR. At the measured 1028-1390ms/spawn, refreshing a 50-tool catalog costs roughly 60s of subprocess churn even though nothing stays resident. So the honest result is two-sided: RESIDENT RESOURCES are bounded by workload/policy (O(K), strong-pass shaped), while ACQUISITION/REFRESH COST is strictly proportional to N (O(N) spawns and O(N) latency). A one-sentence conclusion saying only 'scales with K, not N' would be TRUE OF RESIDENCY AND FALSE OF COST, and #295's own framing ('knows N tools while keeping physical connector-process residency proportional to K') invites exactly that elision. The conclusion must name the axis it is quantifying. I will check this specifically when report.md lands.","created_at":"2026-08-08T18:31:10.005077455Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:Rj4INtResnauuPj2uF3Yb3Z0661Knv1vbBrWaPMatDU","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgbqNUl2DRFUC4HFd7UeMGWePiPjIHsXIxnoRNVkc9JD8AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABA6NvQBTpglN2s/bSr8niuSKMwMUGAO+gNnl6xojiv2grAD6FJjFfWF+j6kRHYZUe62N3FRRL4fXfF+k/bjaAYDw=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:Rj4INtResnauuPj2uF3Yb3Z0661Knv1vbBrWaPMatDU","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgbqNUl2DRFUC4HFd7UeMGWePiPjIHsXIxnoRNVkc9JD8AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQGuldeZyLqVzT0VqatxWH2ci6j9pv/x/vUxmHdDB52BgXCTdoGfRMPXKzolix2mtNdGVBlwdgBDkH2BDii1j3gU="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-Ij1J-phase-1-pre-positioned-auditor-in-flight-divergence","agent_seq":7,"timestamp":"2026-08-08T18:49:48.216590807Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"49266c99-4085-4339-bf60-348c92add444","comment_uuid":"2f46a988-37c7-456e-a2f1-8e3c8f3eab21","author":"pp3g-Ij1J-phase-1-pre-positioned-auditor-in-flight-divergence","content":"[VERIFY] state=verifying completed=Builder checkpoint 3 (18:48:11Z, Tests 1+2) re-derived — no divergence; my pre-registered CONCERN-5 has now MATERIALIZED in the data; 2 new load-related measurement risks raised next=verify Test 3 sweep rows blocker=none\n\nTRIGGER STATE: NO DIVERGENCE. Position 18:26:52 -> 18:48:11 = 21.3min gap. That touched the ~2x band (Builder's own demonstrated cadence is 11.6min, so 2x ~= 23min) and I deliberately did NOT flag it: the lock was continuously held, my own compute model predicted Tests 2+3 at 10-15min of pure subprocess cost, and the Builder has now self-reported a heavily loaded host. Raising staleness there would have been a false positive of exactly the WZSE kind. Recording the near-miss so the cadence bound is calibrated rather than re-litigated.\n\nBUILDER CLAIM (#295 c1224): 'Tests 1+2 PASS: Test1 N=1/5/10/20/50 backend_spawn_count=0, resident-after 0 proxies/0 backends all N; Test2 N=10 condA(no teardown) and condB(immediate teardown) both resident 0/0, backend_spawns=0 (note: host under heavy load avg 15-17, wall-clock durations inflated; counts/residency load-independent). Sync mode = 1 shared async-runtime daemon thread, NOT per-connector. Test3 sweep (20 runs) running in background'.\n\n== ASSESSMENT ==\n[OK] RESTATEMENT CONSISTENCY — Test 1's numbers are restated identically to c1221 (backend_spawn_count=0 all N; resident-after 0/0 all N). Silent drift in restated figures is a classic fabrication tell; there is none here. Positive signal.\n[OK] Test 3 is described as 20 runs, which matches the specified 5 N x 2 modes x 2 teardown design exactly. No silent scope reduction of the sweep.\n[OK] Proxy and backend residency are still being reported SEPARATELY at every checkpoint, per the addendum's never-collapse rule.\n[OK] The load-average disclosure is good practice and the direction of its effect on residency is SAFE: under load a process is slower to exit, so a 2.5s-grace 'resident-after = 0' is if anything a conservative result — it exited despite load avg 15-17. I accept 'residency counts are load-independent' for resident-after.\n\n== MY PRE-REGISTERED CONCERN-5 HAS NOW MATERIALIZED — THIS IS THE MAIN FINDING OF THIS CHECK ==\nTest 2 returned condA (no teardown) and condB (immediate teardown) as IDENTICAL: both resident 0/0, backend_spawns=0. I predicted exactly this before the run, from connection.py:73/79 — list_tools() wraps its work in 'async with MCPClient(...)', so the discovery connection closes itself and there is NOTHING LEFT for the teardown flag to tear down at K=0. Consequences the report MUST carry, or it will mislead:\n (a) The identical rows are the CORRECT result, not a harness bug or a copy-paste error. Say so explicitly, with the mechanism cited, or a reviewer will reasonably suspect duplicated rows.\n (b) The teardown off/on factor is DEGENERATE for every K=0 test (Tests 1, 2, and the whole Test 3 sweep). It is therefore NOT a validated control in those cells — it is an untested variable, because it had no observable surface. Test 6 (and Test 4, K=3) are the only places teardown can actually bite. Presenting a 4-condition sweep as though all four conditions were discriminating would overstate the design's power.\n (c) The strong pass at K=0 is a property of ToolRegistry 0.15.0's disconnect-after-list discovery path, NOT a general validation of lazy-proxy architecture. Scope the architectural conclusion to that mechanism.\n\n== NEW RISKS FROM THE DISCLOSED HOST LOAD (load avg 15-17) ==\n[CONCERN-8 / measurement validity] PEAK IS THE ONE QUANTITY THAT IS *NOT* LOAD-INDEPENDENT. Residency is sampled after a settle window, so load only makes it more conservative. PEAK is sampled DURING acquisition by polling /proc, and a sampler that is itself descheduled under load avg 15-17 will MISS short-lived concurrent peaks and UNDER-report them. So the blanket claim 'counts/residency load-independent' holds for resident-after but does NOT hold for peak. This compounds my still-open CONCERN-6 (peak proxies never reported): the peak column is both absent and, once present, the least trustworthy number in the study under these host conditions. The report should state the /proc sampling interval and treat peak as a LOWER BOUND.\n[CONCERN-9 / cross-agent contamination] PROCESS CLASSIFICATION MUST BE PATH-SCOPED, NOT NAME-SCOPED. The plan classifies processes by /proc cmdline. This repo contains multiple committed copies of proxy.py (research/toolregistry-lazy-mcp/proxy.py and output-schema-drift/proxy.py), and the host is currently running many concurrent agents in sibling worktrees — which is presumably why load avg is 15-17. If the matcher keys on the basename 'proxy.py' rather than the absolute catalog-scale/ path, another agent's unrelated proxy process would be counted into this study's peak and residency figures. Given the measured values are all 0 so far, contamination would only ever inflate them — so the current 0s are safe — but the sweep and the K=3 tests are exposed. The report should state the exact cmdline predicate used.\n\nBoth new concerns are FLAGS for the record, not blockers. Builder should continue; no intervention warranted.","created_at":"2026-08-08T18:49:48.204955311Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:Rj4INtResnauuPj2uF3Yb3Z0661Knv1vbBrWaPMatDU","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgbqNUl2DRFUC4HFd7UeMGWePiPjIHsXIxnoRNVkc9JD8AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAyxB91qZPD+kDICfzSCDv5ZoztmixzlQFhOe4b0FC7dKB/BtDD1rsxKosjnzyJ7LfmGB4sWwhEkSxmJZbYGKZDQ=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:Rj4INtResnauuPj2uF3Yb3Z0661